 "thread": "c1b1nLEDy6g",
 "subject": "Male vs Female Densities",
 "messages_listed": 4,
 "last_activity": "2015-04-19T05:27:28Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "9B6Q-mNZYu4J",
   "from": "Roshan Guharajan",
   "author": "bb519e5dc9ecf883",
   "date": "2015-04-19T01:41:05Z",
   "subject": "Male vs Female Densities",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">I am estimating densities of black bears and have run into some issues. <div><br></div><div>I initially attempted to model males and females differently (as in have different capthist files for males and females), but got similar density estimates for both. I thought maybe it would be worth a try to run sexes combined (single capthist file), but then still got densities that were similar for both sexes.</div><div><br></div><div>Would there be any reason why this happens? It would seem that males would typically be at lower densities.</div><div><br></div><div>I also attempted to run this data with auxiliary telemetry data in MARK and obtained estimates that were very different for males and females.</div><div><br></div><div>Roshan.</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "RGrlEj_FCtMJ",
   "from": "Murray Efford",
   "author": "118cea91a1619106",
   "date": "2015-04-19T01:50:59Z",
   "subject": "Re: Male vs Female Densities",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>The obvious reason is that males and females do have similar densities in your population - and you haven&#39;t really produced evidence that they don&#39;t. If you want a more specific answer please provide detail (data or data summary and code). If you use the &#39;hcov&#39; route for modelling sex-specific detection and sex ratio then exactly the same total density is reported for males and females.<br></div>Murray<br></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Sun, Apr 19, 2015 at 1:41 PM, Roshan Guharajan <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">rosh...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">I am estimating densities of black bears and have run into some issues. <div><br></div><div>I initially attempted to model males and females differently (as in have different capthist files for males and females), but got similar density estimates for both. I thought maybe it would be worth a try to run sexes combined (single capthist file), but then still got densities that were similar for both sexes.</div><div><br></div><div>Would there be any reason why this happens? It would seem that males would typically be at lower densities.</div><div><br></div><div>I also attempted to run this data with auxiliary telemetry data in MARK and obtained estimates that were very different for males and females.</div><span><font color=\"#888888\"><div><br></div><div>Roshan.</div></font></span></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><span><font color=\"#888888\">\r\n\r\n<p></p>\r\n\r\n-- <br>\r\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;secr&quot; group.<br>\r\nTo unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">secrgroup+...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\nFor more options, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/optout\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/optout&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792488227356000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0___lVU75ODXX1Ff85XjPS\">https://groups.google.com/d/optout</a>.<br>\r\n</font></span></blockquote></div><br></div>\r\n"
