     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;padding-left:1ex;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid\"><div><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On 18 April 2015 at 20:50, Murray Efford <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href rel=\"nofollow\" data-email-masked>murray...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br></div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;padding-left:1ex;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid\"><div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;padding-left:1ex;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>The obvious reason is that males and females do have similar densities in your population - and you haven&#39;t really produced evidence that they don&#39;t. If you want a more specific answer please provide detail (data or data summary and code). If you use the &#39;hcov&#39; route for modelling sex-specific detection and sex ratio then exactly the same total density is reported for males and females.<span><font color=\"#888888\"><br></font></span></div><span><font color=\"#888888\">Murray<br></font></span></div></blockquote></div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;padding-left:1ex;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid\"><div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;padding-left:1ex;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid\"><div><div><div><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Sun, Apr 19, 2015 at 1:41 PM, Roshan Guharajan <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href rel=\"nofollow\" data-email-masked>rosh...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br></div></div></div></div></blockquote></div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;padding-left:1ex;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid\"><div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;padding-left:1ex;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid\"><div><div><div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;padding-left:1ex;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid\"><div dir=\"ltr\">I am estimating densities of black bears and have run into some issues. <div><br></div><div>I initially attempted to model males and females differently (as in have different capthist files for males and females), but got similar density estimates for both. I thought maybe it would be worth a try to run sexes combined (single capthist file), but then still got densities that were similar for both sexes.</div><div><br></div><div>Would there be any reason why this happens? It would seem that males would typically be at lower densities.</div><div><br></div><div>I also attempted to run this data with auxiliary telemetry data in MARK and obtained estimates that were very different for males and females.</div><span><font color=\"#888888\"><div><br></div><div>Roshan.</div></font></span></div></blockquote></div></div></div></div></blockquote></div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
